 # Sparse and Efficient Representations
 
-Also called: Sparse NeRF, Local NeRF, Decomposed NeRF
 
 ## Overview
-This family addresses the prohibitive memory and computational costs of dense Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) in large-scale or high-resolution scenes by decomposing the global radiance field into localized, sparse components [S1]. Unlike dense representations that query a single global network for every point, these methods partition space or features to enable parallel training, reduce per-point latency, and allow for efficient mesh extraction [S1, S2, S3]. They distinguish themselves from sibling architectures like [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md) or [Transformer-based Architectures](8998-transformer-based-architectures.md) by explicitly leveraging spatial sparsity or hierarchical decomposition rather than adaptive feature hashing or attention mechanisms alone.
+Sparse and Efficient Representations address the computational and memory bottlenecks of dense Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) by decomposing the scene representation into localized, sparse, or projected components. Unlike monolithic NeRFs that query a single global network for every point, these methods partition the scene spatially, prune empty space, or predict sampling locations directly, enabling scalable training on large-scale environments and faster inference. This family sets itself apart from siblings like [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md) (which adapts network capacity per region) and [SinNeRF](6199-sinnerf.md) (which uses sinusoidal activations for high-frequency detail) by focusing primarily on structural sparsity, spatial partitioning, and sampling efficiency rather than activation functions or adaptive depth.
 
