 ### [Mega-NeRF Spatial Partitioning](8776-mega-nerf-spatial-partitioning.md)
-Decomposes a large-scale scene into spatial cells, each managed by a separate MLP submodule, to enable parallel training and reduce memory usage. It distinguishes itself from dense NeRFs by using spatial locality to limit the training data assigned to each network, and from other sparse methods by employing a fixed 2D grid tessellation for centroid selection rather than dynamic clustering.
+Mega-NeRF decomposes large-scale scenes into spatial cells, each managed by a separate lightweight MLP submodule, to enable parallel training and reduce memory consumption. It assigns each query point to the single closest centroid, allowing independent optimization of local geometry and appearance. Choose this method for city-scale or aerial/drone captures where altitude variance is small and scalability is the primary constraint.
 
 ### [Sparse Radiance Field (SRF) Representation](8876-sparse-radiance-field-srf-representation.md)
-A voxel-based representation of a 3D scene or object that stores density and view-dependent radiance colors in a sparse grid structure. It serves as a compact, differentiable alternative to dense neural radiance fields (NeRFs) and dense voxel grids, enabling efficient optimization and rendering by storing data only for occupied regions. Unlike its siblings, Mega-NeRF Spatial Partitioning and KiloNeRF, which use hierarchical or tree-based structures for scalability, SRF relies on a flat, coordinate-based (COO) sparse array, making mesh extraction straightforward via Marching Cubes.
+A Sparse Radiance Field (SRF) stores radiance and density only in non-empty voxels using a Coordinate Format (COO) list, serving as a compact alternative to dense grids. It enables fast optimization and direct mesh extraction via Marching Cubes. Choose this method when explicit sparse storage and mesh extraction are required, or when memory must be minimized by excluding empty space entirely.
 
 ### [KiloNeRF](8942-kilonerf.md)
-KiloNeRF is a sparse and efficient representation method that decomposes a large, deep Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) into a set of small, shallow MLPs arranged in a 3D grid. It solves the problem of high per-point computational cost in standard NeRFs by using a teacher-student training paradigm to distill knowledge into lightweight local models. It sets itself apart from siblings like Mega-NeRF by focusing on reducing the computational cost per sample via tiny MLPs rather than just spatial partitioning for ray traversal, and from SRF by using explicit MLPs per cell rather than feature vectors.
+KiloNeRF accelerates NeRF rendering by decomposing a large, deep neural network into thousands of small, shallow MLPs arranged in a 3D grid, trained via teacher-student distillation. It focuses on extreme model thinning (tiny MLPs) rather than just spatial partitioning for caching. Choose this method when inference speed is critical and the scene can be effectively approximated by local shallow networks.
+
+### [ProNeRF](9673-pronerf.md)
+ProNeRF uses a Projection-Aware Sampling (PAS) network to predict a small number of high-density 3D points along a ray, enabling high-quality rendering with significantly fewer samples than standard NeRF. It distinguishes itself by using a fixed, small subset of reference views for color-to-ray projection rather than storing all training images. Choose this method when rendering speed is paramount and a fixed set of reference views is available.
+
+### NeRF-XL Multi-GPU Representation Strategy
+NeRF-XL is a parameter distribution strategy for multi-GPU NeRF training where each GPU handles a distinct 3D tiled region with independent hash encoding and density MLP, but shares the color MLP and appearance/camera embeddings across all GPUs [S1]. Hash encoding and density MLPs are independent per GPU, confined within non-overlapping bounding boxes [S1]. The color MLP is shared across all GPUs using Distributed Data Parallel (DDP) to ensure consistent color prediction for novel views, as independent color MLPs would lead to inconsistent predictions for out-of-distribution view directions [S1]. Appearance embeddings are shared; a zero embedding is used for novel-view rendering if enabled during training [S1]. Camera pose embeddings are also shared if camera optimization is enabled [S1]. Unlike independent training where each GPU has its own full NeRF, this method unifies instantiations as a 'single NeRF' with spatially partitioned parameters, increasing model capacity without redundancy [S1]. This approach is applicable to both grid-based and MLP-based NeRFs as long as each instantiation is confined within a non-overlapping bounding box [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The primary motivation for sparse and efficient representations is the $O(N)$ memory and compute complexity of dense NeRFs, which scales poorly with scene size and resolution. Early works like [KiloNeRF](8942-kilonerf.md) and [Mega-NeRF](8776-mega-nerf-spatial-partitioning.md) introduced spatial decomposition to enable training on large-scale datasets (e.g., OpenImages, Mapillary) that exceed GPU memory limits [S1]. [Sparse Radiance Fields (SRF)](8876-sparse-radiance-field-srf-representation.md) further optimized this by using sparse voxel grids to reduce storage and accelerate rendering through direct memory access, facilitating real-time applications and mesh extraction [S1]. These methods form the foundation for modern large-scale NeRF training pipelines.
+The concept of sparse and efficient representations in neural rendering emerged from the need to scale NeRFs beyond small, indoor scenes to large-scale outdoor environments and to reduce the high computational cost of dense MLP queries. Mega-NeRF [S1] and KiloNeRF [S1] were among the early approaches to spatial decomposition and model thinning. Sparse Radiance Fields [S1] leveraged explicit voxel sparsity for memory efficiency. ProNeRF [S1] introduced projection-aware sampling to reduce the number of required samples. NeRF-XL [S1] addressed multi-GPU scaling by partitioning parameters rather than just data. These methods collectively form the foundation for scalable neural radiance field optimization.
 
